On Fri, 26 Jan 2007, Kent Paul Dolan wrote:
> "Arthur J. O'Dwyer" <ajonospam@andrew.cmu.edu> wrote
>
> [needs work]
>
>> I decided that the "It feels comfortable" message
>> was kind of weird.
>
> Well, the whole idea seemed to be that there should
> be _some_ positive message. Perhaps: "you feel
> nurturing" or something similar?

   I considered the whole idea that there should /not/ be some
negative message, which is now the case. (And also that sitting
should speed up hatching, which is now the case.) Everything
else is extra, at no charge to you. :)

> I'd rather the pointless exercise of #sit-ting on a
> sterile egg was the one that happened in silence.
>
> There's not likely to be much clue to the mother
> that the egg is not viable until very late in the
> embryo development, when rustling or the heartbeat
> should become audible.

   How do you know? :)  NetHack has so many instances of
even less canonical telepathy (e.g., knowing that your
distant pet is starving) that a little mother-child
telepathy doesn't seem out of place.

> That "splat" seems misplaced.
>
> Just because you sit on the wrong flavor egg doesn't
> make that egg's shell any weaker.

   Sure, it doesn't make the shell weaker; but it breaks
the egg. If you don't believe me, go sit on an egg yourself
right now and mark the results.

> Any natural egg sitter should be able to hatch any
> egg, within size constraints at least.

   I briefly considered that a blue dragon shouldn't break
red dragon eggs, and vice versa, but decided it would be too
much work to code for no gain in gameplay. (Maybe I could
have done it in three extra lines with a test of something
against S_DRAGON, but I didn't care to find out.)
   I seriously considered homages to "Horton Hatches the Egg"
and/or "Flap your Wings!", but both source mythoses (mythi?)
involve generic "birds", and the only bird NetHack has is the
decidedly un-motherly raven, so I let it pass.

   The idea of splatting the egg is that you're big, therefore
presumably a little clumsy; and you're trying to sit on a type
of egg that you were never meant to sit on, therefore you
don't really know what to do, and in shuffling your rear end
around, you might accidentally squash it.
   I think the only egg-laying creatures big enough to splat an
egg by sitting on it are dragons, anyway, and they must have
pretty solid rear ends --- no masses of feathers to cushion an
egg.

-Arthur


